o's dragon sirax and a  small family ceremony  Otto and the rest of the small Council  immediately bring up the succession  which prompts a meltdown from viserys  who's in mourning but as ill-timed as it  might have been it put some things into  motion Otto has a heart to heart with  his daughter Allison where he encourages  her to go and try to comfort the King  Damon is comforting himself and his  brothers in the gold cloaks out of  brothel and what could best be described  as an orgy I wouldn't say he was  celebrating though it's actually a  pretty great small performance where you  can't tell if he feels bad about his  brother or if he might hate himself for  the life that he's chosen to live it  certainly hints that there might be more  to the character but he makes a remark  about the deceased being the heir for a  day that Otto is quick to run and tell  the king about and that earns him a  ticket out of King's Landing he leaves  with massaria on the back of his dragon  craxies and his brother makes a decision  he calls for his daughter and they talk  in front of the skull of valerian the  black dread the dragon that aegon the  Conqueror rode and the one that viserys  rode himself until it died of old age he  tells her that he's making her his Heir  and then the show adds a bit of  information that George R.R Martin has  hinted at but notably didn't mention in  any of his books including the huge fake  history about House Targaryen that this  show is based on he lets her in on a  family secret that is only passed from  King to Heir when he came to Westeros  aegon had a dream he first saw the end  of the world of men it is to begin with  a terrible winter gusting out of the  distant North aegon saw absolute  Darkness riding on those winds and  whatever dwells within will destroy the  house of the living when this great  winter comes all of Westeros must stand  against it a Targaryen must be seated on  the throne to unite the realm aegon  called his dream The Song of Ice and  Fire and this secret has been passed  from King to air since his time and now  you must promise to carry it and protect  it I've known about this for a while and  I'm still not sure how I feel about it  on the one hand it connects the two  series and if it's coming from the  author himself then I suppose on some  level it's not up for debate on the  other it sort of changes my thoughts on  who the targaryens are and why they  conquered Westeros in the first place  it's not to say that both of those  things can't exist inside this universe  it was just sort of a surprise and so I  suppose we'll have to wait and see what  they do with it what I liked more here  was his warning about the idea of them  controlling the dragons being an  illusion he says that they're a power  that men should have never trifled with  and one that brought Valeria to its Doom  even though his earlier dream about his  son being born with an iron Crown didn't  pan out the way he thought it would  maybe viserys is a little wiser than we  give him credit for either way the king  calls his Lords to the throne room and  declares raniera his Heir you see her  smile at her father turned to look at  the crowd and then it cuts to Black  while it looks as though the weight of  the realm is resting on her shoulders  and in the black you hear the sound of  dragons I thought this was a really  promising introduction to a story that  if I'm being honest wasn't one of my  favorites in the greater ice and fire  Universe I thought it was great as a  historical footnote about something  tragic that has to happen but wasn't  sure how it would translate into a  multi-season series that is essentially  becomes a horrors of War Story going in  I was thinking the show needed to do two  things established itself is distinct be  something different than the show that  came before it while leaning into some  of the things that Drew us to that in  the first place also it needed to find a  way to make us 